 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Performance:** On the Building Scene (5,000 iterations), Drone-NeRF achieves an average PSNR of 17.401, outperforming MipNeRF (16.637) and Instant-NGP (17.179) [S1]. It also outperforms these baselines in SSIM and LPIPS metrics [S1].
-*   **Hardware:** Evaluated on Ubuntu 20.04, Nvidia 4070ti GPU (12GB VRAM), Intel i5-13600KF CPU, 32GB RAM [S1].
-*   **Complexity:** The method uses a block parallel training process, training each sub-block independently. This reduces the computational requirement for rendering by using only essential sub-bounding boxes [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** High iteration counts (e.g., 30,000) may lead to over-fitting if not carefully managed [S1]. The method is specifically designed for drone-view overhead scenes and may not generalize to other viewpoints without adaptation.
+*   **Time Complexity:** Training is performed in parallel for each sub-block, reducing total wall-clock time. The use of hash coding in the initial stage facilitates rapid queries [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Depends on the number of sub-bounding boxes and the MLP size. The MLP has 8 layers of 256 units and 2 layers of 128 units [S1].
+*   **Measured Performance:** Evaluated on the Mill 19 dataset (Building and Rubble scenes) [S1].
+    *   **Hardware:** Ubuntu 20.04, Nvidia 4070ti GPU (12GB VRAM), Intel i5-13600KF CPU, 32GB RAM [S1].
+    *   **Building Scene (5,000 iterations):**
+        *   Drone-NeRF Average PSNR: 17.401 [S1].
+        *   MipNeRF Average PSNR: 16.637 [S1].
+        *   Instant-NGP Average PSNR: 17.179 [S1].
+    *   Drone-NeRF outperforms MipNeRF and Instant-NGP in PSNR, SSIM, and LPIPS metrics for both 5,000 and 30,000 iterations on the Building Scene [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Handles unbounded space via spatial contraction, mitigates shadow artifacts from vertical faces, and corrects exposure discrepancies using appearance embeddings [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires careful partitioning of the scene into sub-bounding boxes. Over-fitting can occur if iterations are too high, necessitating careful tuning of iteration counts (5,000 or 30,000) [S1].
 
 ## Background
-Drone-NeRF was introduced to address the specific challenges of 3D scene reconstruction from large-scale drone surveys, such as the Mill 19 dataset (Building and Rubble scenes) [S1]. It builds on the NeRF framework but incorporates specific modifications for aerial imagery, including exposure balancing and shadow mitigation [S1].
+Drone-NeRF was introduced to address the specific challenges of 3D reconstruction from aerial drone imagery, such as large-scale unbounded scenes and varying camera exposure [S1]. It builds upon the NeRF framework [Parent] by incorporating techniques for efficient large-scale rendering and photometric consistency. The method was evaluated on the Mill 19 dataset, demonstrating superior performance compared to MipNeRF and Instant-NGP [S1].
 
